<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Dobruja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cadrilater</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> is the southern part of the Dobruja region between the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, today in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Part of Bulgaria from 1878, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a> in 1913 and returned to Bulgaria by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> in 1940. Its chief towns are Dobrich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Silistra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja.jpg" alt="Southern Dobruja" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Made by Bogdan. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dobrogea-cadrilater.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
